    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d401">

                <pb facs="0453" n="345" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg345"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/8105: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Mayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-01-24T12:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 24,
                                1927—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received January 24—6:17
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>64. 1. Following from American consul general, Hankow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“January 22, 3 p.m. British banks, shipping companies and firms will
                            reopen Monday. This should materially relieve the very tense situation
                            that has prevailed for nearly three weeks. O’Malley<anchor n="77"
                                xml:id="d401-f1"/> presented proposals to Ch’en<anchor n="78"
                                xml:id="d401-f2"/> yesterday, the nature of which can be had from
                            the British Minister.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>2. Proposals referred to are doubtless consonant with the terms British
                    Government are prepared to offer to Nationalists as described in British
                    Ambassador’s note of January 19 (see Department’s 17, January 20, 4 p.m.<anchor
                        n="79" xml:id="d401-f3"/>)</p>
                <p>3. As soon as British Minister is directly informed by O’Malley that the latter
                    has presented proposals to Ch’en he intends to deal similarly with Chang Tso-lin
                        regime<anchor n="80" xml:id="d401-f4"/> as regards Tientsin British
                    Concession.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d401-f1" n="77" xml:id="d401-f1-content">
                <p>Owen St. Clair O’Malley, acting counselor of the British Legation at Peking,
                    negotiating agreements with the Nationalist authorities for the future
                    administration of British Concessions at Hankow and Kiukiang, January and
                    February 1927.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d401-f2" n="78" xml:id="d401-f2-content">
                <p>Eugene Ch’en, Minister for Foreign Affairs in the Nationalist Government at
                    Hankow.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d401-f3" n="79" xml:id="d401-f3-content">
                <p>See footnote 76, <ref target="#pg344">p. 344</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d401-f4" n="80" xml:id="d401-f4-content">
                <p>The Government at Peking.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
